No one patches old clothes with new fabric, because the fabric will absorb and take from the old clothes, and the patch will become bigger.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins; otherwise, the wineskins will burst, the wine will spill, and the wineskins will be ruined. But new wine is put into new wineskins, and both are preserved.” The little girl is revived, and the woman who had been suffering from bleeding is healed (Mark 5:21-43; Luke 8:40-56)
When Jesus was speaking to them, a man from the Jewish leaders came and bowed before him, saying, "My daughter is still dead. Come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
And Jesus got up and they followed him, he and his disciples.
There was a woman suffering from bleeding from the folds of her year, it came from the end and the tip of her finger was swollen,
Because of her, she said in her heart: "If only we could just mess with him to get revenge."
The house of Jesus and its healer said to her: "Have faith, my daughter, your faith has healed you." Immediately, the woman was well.
And when Jesus arrived at the house of the Jewish leader and saw the crowd shouting in the courts and the people causing a commotion,
He said: "Move away! The girl didn't die, she's just sleeping."
And when the people left, he entered and grabbed the girl by her hand, and she stood up.
The news about this thing spreads all over that area. Jesus heals two blind men.
And as Jesus passed by, two blind men followed him, shouting, "Son of David, have mercy on us!"
When he entered the house, the two blind men came to him. Jesus said to them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" They said to him, "Yes, Lord!"
Jesus touched their eyes and said, "Let it be done for you according to your faith."
And their eyes were opened. And Jesus warned them, "Be careful, let no one know!"
But they went out and spread the news about him all over that region. Jesus heals a man with leprosy
And when the two blind men went out, there was a group that brought him a club, his neighbor was a devil.
Jesus cast out the demon, and the man began to speak. Everyone was amazed and said, "We have never seen anything like this in Israel!"
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the power of the prince of demons." Jesus has compassion on the people.
And Jesus went around all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God, and healing every disease and sickness among the people.
When people saw them, they felt sorry for them because they were worried and tired like sheep without a pasture.
And he said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few."
Maybe they should ask the master of the harvest to send workers for his harvest.
The Twelve Apostles (Mark 3:13-19; Luke 6:12-16)
He called the twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits to cast them out and to heal people of all diseases and pains.
And these are the names of the twelve apostles: the first is Simon, who is called Peter, and his brother Andrew, and James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John,
And Philips and Bartholomew, and Thomas and Matthew the tax collector, and James son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus,
Simon who is jealous of his country, and Judas Iscariot who is going to betray Jesus. Jesus sends out the twelve apostles (Mark 6:7-13; Luke 9:1-6)
Jesus sent out these twelve and instructed them: "Do not go to any of the towns of the other peoples, and do not enter any of the Samaritan cities,
But go after the lost sheep of the house of Israel.
And as you go, preach and say that the kingdom of the heavens is near.
Heal the sick, raise the dead, heal those with leprosy, cast out demons. You received without payment, give without payment.
Do not carry gold, silver, or copper in your belts,
No bag, no clothes, no sandals, no stick, because the servant deserves what he eats.
And any city or village you enter, look for someone worthy to enter his house and stay with him until you leave.
And when they enter the house, they greet the people inside.
And if the hopes of that house deserve it, let your peace come upon them, but if they do not deserve it, your peace will return to you.
And if anyone does not accept you or does not listen to your words, shake the dust off your feet when you leave that house or that city.
I tell you the truth: it will be like the situation of the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah on the Day of Judgment, to be better than the situation of that city!» The persecution that will happen (Mark 13:9-13; Luke 21:12-17)
I send you like sheep in the middle of the wolves, so be alert like the fox, and good like the dove.
Be careful of the people because of them, so they can hand you over to the Jewish council and whip you in their synagogues,
And you shake for the rulers and kings for my sake, so that you bear witness before them and before other peoples.
When they greet you, don't think about how to speak or what to say, because at that moment, the words you want to say will get stuck.
For your sake, it's not you who should be talking, but let your father speak about your way.
The brother is about to hand over his brother to death, the father his son, and the children search for their parents and kill them,
And all the people will hate you because of my name. But whoever perseveres to the end will be saved.
And when they persecute you in one city, flee to another city. Truly, I tell you: they will not finish going through all the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not greater than his teacher, and the servant is not greater than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher and the servant like his master. If the owner of the house is called Beelzebub, how much more will his household be called? Fear God (Luke 12:2-7)
So don't be afraid of them. There is nothing hidden that will not be revealed, and there is nothing concealed that will not be known.
What I say to you in the dark, say it in the light. What is whispered in your ears, proclaim it from the rooftops.
And do not fear when they kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather fear Him who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Aren't two sparrows sold for a franc? And they don't fall to the ground without your Father in heaven knowing about it.
Even the hair on your head is all counted.
Don't be afraid, you are better than many birds!
If he acknowledges me before people, I will acknowledge him before my Father in heaven.
"But whoever denies me before others, I will also deny before my Father in heaven." Jesus and the world (Luke 12:51-53; 14:26-27)
Don't think that I came to bring peace to the land; I did not come to bring peace but to bring a sword.
I came to distinguish between the boy and his father, the girl and her mother, and the daughter-in-law and her mother-in-law.
And the enemies of a person will be his desires.
Whoever loves their father or mother more than me does not deserve me. And whoever loves their son or daughter more than me does not deserve me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever wants to save their life will lose it, but whoever loses their life for my sake will find it." The Reward (Mark 9:36-41)
Whoever accepts you accepts me, and whoever accepts me accepts the one who sent me.
Whoever accepts a prophet for his sake, a prophet will come to receive the reward of a prophet. And whoever accepts a righteous man for his sake, a righteous man will come to receive the reward of a righteous man.
And he doesn't even give one of these little ones a glass of cold water because he's my student. I'm telling you the truth, his reward will not be lost.
Disciples of John the Baptist (Luke 7:18-35)
And when Jesus finished instructing his twelve disciples, he went from that place to teach and preach in the towns in that area.
And John, while he was in prison, heard about the works of Christ, so he sent to Him through his disciples.
And they said: "Are you the one coming, or should we wait for someone else?"
Jesus answered them, "Go and tell John what you have seen and heard,"
# Isaiah 35:5-6; Psalm 61:1; Luke 4:18-19 The blind see, the lame walk, those with leprosy are healed, the deaf hear, the dead rise, and the poor are preached the gospel.
And why should he not doubt it!
When John's disciples left, Jesus began to speak to the people about John and said: "What did you go out to see in the wilderness? A reed shaken by the wind?"
So what did you come out to see? To see a man wearing expensive clothes? Those who wear expensive clothes are in the palaces of kings!
Well, what did you come out to see? To see a prophet? Yes, indeed, and I tell you: greater than a prophet.
# Malachi 3:1; Mark 1:2 This is what is written in the Holy Bible: "Behold, I send my messenger before you, who will prepare your way."
I tell you the truth, no one among those born of women is greater than John the Baptist, but the one who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than him.
From the days of John the Baptist until today, the kingdom of heaven has been advancing with power, and strong people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
And if you want to believe it, he is Elijah who is going to come.
Whoever has ears, let them hear!
In Bshnoua, who does this generation resemble? They are like kids sitting in the market calling out to each other:
We hummed for you and you didn't dance, We lamented for you and you didn't cry.
John neither eats nor drinks; you said: "He is possessed by a demon."
When the Son of Man comes, eating and drinking, you say, "Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is proved right by her children.» Cities that did not repent (Luke 10:13-15)
And from a distance, Jesus began to rebuke the cities where he had performed his great miracles, and they did not repent.
He said: "Woe to you, Korzin! Woe to you, the house of Sidon! If the great miracles that you perform in your midst were performed in Tyre and Sidon, then surely they would have repented long ago, put on sackcloth, and sat in ashes."
But I tell you: the condition of the cities of Tyre and Sidon will be better than your condition on the Day of Judgment.
And you, Kfar Nahum! Are you going to rise to the sky? You are going to fall into the abyss. Because if the great miracles that were done in you had been done in the city of Sodom, it would still be standing today.
But I tell you: the condition of the land of Sodom will be better than your condition on the day of judgment.» Jesus rejoices (Luke 10:21-22)
At that time Jesus said: "Thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and understanding, and revealed them to little children."
Yes, Dad, this is how you wanted it.
Dad gave me everything. And no one knows that the son was the father, and no one knows that the father was the son, and everyone chooses the son to show him.
I know that you are tired and burdened, and I am here to relieve you.
They tipped the scale in my favor and learned from me that I am kind and humble, and now you will find peace in your souls.
My balance isn't steady, and my load is light.
Saturday (Mark 2:23-28; Luke 6:1-5)
At that time, Jesus passed through the grainfields on the Sabbath, and his disciples were hungry. So they began to pick some heads of grain and eat them.
But when the Pharisees saw this, they said, "Look! Your disciples are doing something that is not lawful to do on the Sabbath."
# I Samuel 21:1-6; Luke 24:9 Jesus said to them: "My brother, haven't you read what David did, he and those with him when he was hungry?"
How did he enter the house of God and eat the communion bread, which was not lawful for him or those with him to eat, but was lawful for the priests to eat?
# Numbers 28:9-10 Brother, haven't you read in the law how the priests in the temple on the Sabbath break the Sabbath commandment and no one blames them?
But I say to you: There is one here greater than the temple.
# Hosea 6:6; Matthew 9:13 And if you had known what this verse means: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice." Then you would not have judged those who are not to be judged.
"I am the Son of Man, Lord of the Sabbath." The man with the paralyzed hand is healed (Mark 3:1-6; Luke 6:6-11)
And Jesus went to that place and entered their synagogue.
And there was a man there with a paralyzed hand. People asked him: "Is it permissible for one to heal on a Saturday?" to find something to accuse him of.
Jesus said to them: "Which one of you, if he has a sheep that falls into a pit on the Sabbath day, will not take hold of it and lift it out of the pit?"
And how much more valuable is a person than knowledge! So that's why it's permissible to do good deeds on Saturday.
And immediately Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand!" And the man stretched it out, and it was completely restored, just like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired against Jesus on how to kill him. Jesus is the servant chosen by God.
And Jesus knew this and left that place. Many people followed him, and he healed them all.
And he ordered them not to tell anyone about it.
And this happened to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah said:
# Isaiah 42:1-4 Here is my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights; I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will bring justice to the nations.
He neither struggles nor cries out, and no one in the streets hears his voice.
A sick fortress cannot be broken, and a lit wick cannot be extinguished. Until justice prevails,
And the peoples will have hope in His name. Jesus and Beelzebul (Mark 3:20-30; Luke 11:14-23; 12:10)
At that time, a blind man was brought to him, and his name was Bakoush, living in Shaitan. Jesus saw him, and he began to speak and see.
And all the people were amazed and said, "Could this be the son of David?"
But the Pharisees and the teachers of the law, when they heard this, said, "He is casting out demons by Beelzebul, the prince of demons."
And Jesus knew what they were thinking, and he said to them: "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself cannot stand."
If Satan were to cast out Satan, he would be divided against himself, so how could his kingdom stand?
And if Anna goes out in the alleys with Beelzebub, who will your students go out with? That is why they are the ones who will judge you.
But if we are led by the Spirit of God, then surely the kingdom of God has control over you.
And how can someone enter the house of a strong man and unpack his belongings, unless he first shoulders the strong man, and then he can unpack the things in his house?
Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not unite with me divides.
This is why I tell you, the sins and disbelief of people can all be forgiven, but the disbelief against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
"And whoever says a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever says a word against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, neither in this world nor in the world to come." The Tree and Its Fruit (Luke 6:43-45)
Either let the tree be good and its fruit be good, or let the tree be bad and its fruit be bad. The tree is known by its fruit.
Oh sons of Al-Lifai, how can you say good words while you are wicked? The tongue speaks what is in the heart.
A good person brings out the good things from their good heart, and a bad person brings out the bad things from their bad heart.
Your transmission: Every empty word people say will be held accountable for on the Day of Judgment.
With your words, you appear innocent, and with your words, you are judged." The Pharisees ask for a miracle (Mark 8:11-12; Luke 11:29-32)
At that time, some of the Jewish scribes and Pharisees said to Jesus, "Teacher, we want you to show us a miracle."
But he answered them: "A wicked and adulterous generation seeks a miracle, but no miracle will be given to them except the miracle of the Prophet Jonah."
# John 1:17 And just as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the whale, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.
# Jonah 3:5 The people of Nineveh repented so that they would stand with this generation on the day of judgment and be judged, because they repented when Jonah warned them, and here is someone greater than Jonah.
# I Kings 10:1-10; II Chronicles 9:1-12 and the Queen of the South will rise with this generation on the day of judgment and condemn it, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and here is someone greater than Solomon!” The return of the unclean spirit (Luke 11:24-26)
When the cursed spirit leaves a person, it passes through places without water looking for rest, but it doesn't find any place at all.
At that time he says: "I will return to my home that I left." And when he returns, he finds it empty, swept, and organized.
He goes around and brings with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter that person and dwell in him, and his condition becomes worse than it was before. And so it will be with this evil generation." The mother of Jesus and his brothers (Mark 3:31-35; Luke 8:19-21)
While Jesus was still speaking to the people, his mother and brothers came and stood outside, wanting to speak with him.
Someone said: "Your mother and siblings are standing outside; they want to talk to you."
But the man he spoke to answered: "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
He stretched out his hand towards his students and said: "They are my mother and my siblings."
Anyone who wills my father's will in heaven is my brother, my sister, and my mother.
The Parable of the Sower (Mark 4:1-9; Luke 8:4-8)
And Jesus went out rich that day by himself and sat by the lake.
Many people gathered around him, until he got on a boat, and everyone stood on the shore.
And he told them many things in proverbs: "The farmer went out to plant."
And when he was planting, some seeds fell into the valley, and the birds came and ate them.
And there is another seed that fell on rocky ground, with not much soil, yet it sprouted immediately because the soil was not abundant.
But when the sun rose, it burned and dried up because it has no veins.
And another seed fell among the thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked it.
And there are other seeds that fell on fertile soil and spread widely; some brought bitter waters more than what they planted, some brought sixty, and some brought thirty.
"Whoever has ears, let them hear!" Why Jesus speaks in parables (Mark 4:10-12; Luke 8:9-10)
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus and said to him, "Why do you speak to them in parables?"
And He answered them: "You have been given the ability to know the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens, but they have not been given this ability."
Those who have, will have more taken away from them and will end up with a lot. But those who have nothing, even what little they have will be taken from them.
That's why I speak to them in proverbs, because they are blind, don't see or hear, and don't understand.
# Isa 6:9-10; Mar 8:18; Joh 12:39-41; Act 28:26-27 The prophecy of Isaiah was fulfilled in them, which says: You will hear but never understand, and you will look but never see.
The people have hardened their hearts, closed their ears, and shut their eyes, so they do not see with their eyes, do not hear with their ears, and do not understand with their hearts, and they come back to me so I can heal them.
But you are the health of my eyes because of which I see, and the delight of my ears because of which I hear.
I tell you the truth: Many prophets and righteous men wished to see what you see but did not see it, and to hear what you hear but did not hear it.” Interpretation of the Parable of the Sower (Mark 4:13-20; Luke 8:11-15)
So listen carefully to what is meant by the farmer's proverb:
Whenever anyone hears the word of the kingdom of God and does not understand it, the evil one comes and snatches away what was sown in their heart; this is what was sown along the path.
And he who sows on the rocky ground is the one who hears the word and immediately receives it with joy:
But he has no backbone inside, he endures a little, and when hard times or persecution come because of the word, he immediately falls apart.
Why plant in the midst of thorns? It is the one who hears the word. But the worries of the world and the love of wealth choke the word, and it becomes barren.
The one who plants in fertile soil is the one who hears the word and understands it, and produces a lot of seed; some produce a hundred times what they plant, another sixty, and another thirty." The parable of the sower
And Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.
But when people were asleep, his enemy came and planted weeds in the middle of the wheat and left.
And when the seed sprouted and produced wheat, weeds grew along with it.
And the servants of the landowner came and said to him: "Oh our master, didn't you plant good seeds in your land? So where did the weeds come from?"
He told them: "One of my enemies did this." The servants said: "So, do you want us to go and gather the boys?"
But he said: "No, don't harvest the wheat when you are gathering the flowers.
Let the wheat and the tares grow together until the time of harvest, and at that time we will say to the reapers, "First gather the tares and bind them to be burned, but gather the wheat into my barn."» Like the mustard seed and the yeast (Mark 4:30-32; Luke 13:18-19)
And he gave them another example, saying: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field."
She is the smallest of all the seeds, but when she grew up, she became the largest of all the vegetables, and she became a tree, to the extent that the birds of the sky come and nest in her.»
And another said: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and mixed into three measures of flour until the whole dough was leavened."
All these things Jesus said to the people in parables. He did not tell them anything without a parable.
# Psalm 78:2 This thing was to be fulfilled that the prophet said: "I will speak with my mouth in parables, and declare dark sayings from the beginning." Interpretation of the parable of the swan
At that time, Jesus left the people and went into the house, and his disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds."
Answer them: "The one who sowed the good seed is I, the Son of Man,
The seed is the world, and the good seed are the children of the kingdom of God, and the weeds are the children of the evil one,
And the enemy he planted is pestilence, the harvest is the end of times, and the harvesters are the angels.
And exactly as the straw gathers and burns in the fire, so will it happen at the end of times.
I am the Son of Man to send my angels, and they will gather the wicked and those who do evil in my kingdom.
And they throw them into the midst of the fire, where there will be weeping and the gnashing of teeth.
And at that time, the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of Bohem. Whoever has ears, let him hear!» Like treasure, diamonds, and the net
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure buried in a field, which a man found and hid, and out of joy, he went and sold everything he had and bought that field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
He found a valuable piece of gold, went and sold everything he had, and bought it.
The kingdom of heaven is like a net that was thrown into the sea and gathered many kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen took it out to the shore, sat down, put the good fish in boxes, and threw away the bad ones.
And this is what will happen at the end of times: the angels will come and separate the wicked from among the righteous.
And they throw them into the midst of the fire, and there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.» The new and the old
And Jesus said to his disciples: "Do you understand all this?" They answered: "Yes."
And He said to them: "This is why every scribe who becomes a disciple in the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out of his treasure new things and old things."
And when Jesus finished this parable, he went away from that place,
And as he began to teach them in their synagogue, they were amazed and said, "Where did this man get this wisdom and these miracles?"
Isn't this the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother's name Mary? And aren't his brothers Jacob, Joseph, Simon, and Judah?
"And aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all this from?"
And this is how they doubted him. Jesus said to them: "A prophet is honored everywhere, in his own country and in his own house."
And he did not perform many miracles there because they did not believe in him.
The Death of John the Baptist (Mark 6:14-29; Luke 9:7-9)
At that time, King Herod heard the news about Jesus,
And he said to his servants: "This is John the Baptist risen from the dead, that's why he is performing miracles."
Before Herod, John was arrested, bound, and put in prison because of Herodias, the wife of Philip his brother.
Because John said: "It's forbidden for you to take it."
And they wanted to kill him, but they feared the people because they thought John was a prophet.
But when Herod's birthday came, Herodias's daughter danced at the party, and Herod was pleased,
That's why he promised her and swore to give her anything she asks for.
And following her mother's instructions, she said to Herod: "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter!"
And the king was saddened, as if something he loved was about to be taken away, because of the oath he swore and the guests.
And Shakun was sent, John’s head was cut off in prison.
They put his head on a plate and gave it to the girl, but she took it to her mother.
John's disciples came, took his body, and buried it, and then went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand men (Mark 6:30-44; Luke 9:10-17; John 6:1-14)
When Jesus heard about this, he left that place in a boat and went to a place where there was no one. But when the people heard about him, they followed him on foot from the city.
When he got off the boat, he saw many people, so he felt sorry for them and healed their illnesses.
In the evening, his disciples came to him and said: "The day is over, and this place is empty. Send the people away so they can go to the villages and buy something to eat."
Jesus said: "They don't need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They told him: "We only have five loaves of bread and two fish."
Jesus said: "Bring them to me here."
He ordered the people to sit down on the grass, took the five loaves and two fish, lifted his eyes to the sky, blessed and broke the loaves, and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
And they all ate and were satisfied. They carried ten baskets full of leftovers.
And the people who ate were about five thousand men, besides women and children. Jesus walks on water (Mark 6:45-52; John 6:15-21)
And then exactly he orders his students to get on the boat and go ahead to the opposite side of the lake until he sends people to go.
After the people left, he went up alone to the mountain to pray. After the sun had set, he was still there alone.
But at that time, the boat was very far from the shore and the waves overwhelmed it, because the wind was against it.
And before dawn, Jesus came to them walking on the water.
But the students were afraid when they saw him walking on the water and said: "This is an illusion!" and they began to scream out of fear.
But Jesus immediately spoke to them and said, "Take courage. It is I; do not be afraid!"
And Peter answered, "Lord, if it is you, command me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said: "Come." Then Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water and came to Jesus.
But when the wind blew strongly, he got scared, he almost drowned, and he cried out: "Oh Lord, save me!"
At the time, Jesus stretched out his hand, took hold of him, and said, "You of little faith, why did you doubt?"
And when they set out in the boat, the wind died down.
And in the boat, they bowed down to him and said, "Truly, you are the Son of God!" Jesus heals many people who have many diseases (Mark 6:53-56)
Jesus and his disciples left that place and came to the region of Gennesaret.
And when the hopes of that country knew Jesus, they spread his news throughout that entire region and brought to him all the people who were sick.
They asked him to let them touch even the tip of the dabshoo. And those who did, all of them got healed.
The Customs of the Ancestors (Mark 7:1-13)
Then the Pharisees and the Jewish scribes came to Jesus from Jerusalem, and said:
"Why don't your students respect the customs of the elders? And why don't they wash their hands when they eat?"
Answer them: "And why don't you obey God's command and firmly hold on to your customs?"
Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16; Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9 God said: "Honor your father and your mother, and whoever curses his father or his mother shall surely be put to death."
But you say if someone says to his father or mother: "What I used to help you with, I have given it to God, he is not obliged to honor his father."
And so you have rendered the word of God void by your traditions.
O hypocrites, Isaiah's prophecy and killing truly foretold about you:
Psalm 29:13 This people honor me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.
They worship in vain and teach human commandments.» What defiles a person (Mark 7:14-23)
And Jesus called the people and said to them: "Listen and understand
It is not what enters the mouth that defiles a person, but what comes out of the mouth that defiles a person.
His disciples came to him and said, "Do you realize that the Pharisees got upset when they heard this?"
Answer them: "No tree was planted by my Father in heaven to be uprooted."
Stay away from them! They are blind leading the blind. And if a blind man is leading another blind man, they will both fall into a pit.»
But Peter said, "Explain this parable to us."
And Jesus said: "My brothers, do you still not understand?"
Man, don’t you see that anything that goes into the mouth ends up in the stomach, and eventually comes out of the body?
But the need that comes out of the mouth comes from the heart, and this is what defiles a person.
The heart does not bring evil thoughts, murder, adultery, corruption, theft, false testimony, and disbelief.
These are the ones who defile a person. But food eaten with unwashed hands does not defile a person." The faith of the Canaanite woman (Mark 7:24-30)
And Jesus went from that place and came to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
And then a Canaanite woman from that area came and shouted: "Oh sir, son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is possessed by a demon, and it torments her greatly."
But he did not answer her with a single word. Then his students came and begged him, saying: "Give her whatever she wants so she leaves us alone, she is following us and shouting!"
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
But she came and got on her mounts in front of him and said: "Oh sir, help me!"
And he answered her: "It's not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
She said: "Yes, sir! Even dogs eat the scraps that fall from their owner's table."
Jesus answered her, "Woman, your faith is great! Let it be done for you as you wish." And immediately her daughter was healed. Jesus heals many people.
And Jesus went from there and crossed over to the shore of the Sea of Galilee, and went up on the mountain and sat there.
Many people came to Java and brought with them paralyzed people, the sick, the blind, the mute, and many others, and they placed them in front of Saqia while he healed them.
And the people marveled as the lame walked, the lepers were cleansed, the paralyzed walked, and the blind saw. And they glorified the God of Israel. Jesus fed four thousand men (Mark 8:1-10)
And Jesus called his disciples and said to them: "The people are hungry, because they have stayed with me for three days without food. And I do not want to send them away hungry so that they do not faint on the way."
The students said: "Where are we going to get bread in this wilderness? Will it be enough for all these people?"
And Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said, "Seven loaves and a few small fish."
And Jesus commanded the people to sit on the ground,
And he took the seven loaves and the fish cakes, gave thanks to God, broke them, and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
And they all ate until they were full, and they took what was left—seven large full sacks.
They said there were four thousand men, not including women and children.
And after the people left, he went up into the boat and headed to the Majdal area.
Signs of the times (Mark 8:11-13; Luke 12:54-56)
The Pharisees and the Sadducees came to test Jesus, and they asked him to show them a miracle from heaven.
And Jesus answered them, "When evening comes, you say, 'The weather will be good, for the sky is red.'"
And in the morning they say: "There is rain today, because the sky is dark red." You know how to interpret the state of the sky, but you do not know how to interpret the signs of the times.
An evil and adulterous generation seeks a miracle, but no miracle will be given to it except the miracle of Jonah." And he left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees (Mark 8:14-21)
When the students arrived at the opposite shore, they forgot to bring bread with them,
Jesus said to them: "Wake up and be careful of the yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees."
The students discussed among themselves and said: "That's because we don't have bread."
But Jesus knew this and said: "You of little faith, why do you argue among yourselves and say, 'We have no bread'?"
Man, you still don’t get it? Don’t you remember the five loaves that were shared among the five thousand and how many big baskets were left over?
And not the seven loaves of the four thousand, how many large baskets did you fill?
How come you can't understand that I'm not talking about bread? Pay attention to the yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees!
At that time, they understood that he was telling them to beware of the teaching of the Pharisees and the Sadducees, not the yeast of bread. Peter testifies that Jesus is the Christ (Mark 8:27-30; Luke 9:18-21)
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples: "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered him: "Some say that you are John the Baptist, others say that you are Elijah, and others say that you are Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
He said to them: "So, who are you talking about me?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
And Jesus said: "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! It is not flesh and blood that revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
And I tell you: You are Peter, which means a large stone, and on this stone I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
"I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven; whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."
At that time, he instructed his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks for the first time about his death and resurrection (Mark 8:31; 9:1; Luke 9:22-27).
And at that time, Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem, suffer at the hands of the elders, chief priests, and scribes of the Jews, be killed, and on the third day rise again.
And Peter shook Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "Far be it from you, Lord! This shall never happen to you!"
But Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a hindrance to me, because you are thinking not as God does, but as humans do."
At that time, Jesus said to his disciples: "If anyone wants to follow me, let him deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me,
Whoever wants to save their life will lose it, and whoever loses their life for my sake will gain it.
What is the use of a person if they gain the whole world but lose their life? And what is the thing for which a person would sacrifice their life?
I am the Son of Man, to come with my angels in the glory of the Father, and at that time I will reward each person according to what they have done.
I tell you the truth: Some of those standing here are not here to see death before they see me, the Son of Man, coming in my kingdom.»
Jesus reveals His spirit to His disciples (Mark 9:2-13; Luke 9:28-36)
And after six days, Jesus took with him Peter, James, and John his brother, and he went up with them alone to a high mountain,
And it changed before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his beard became white like light.
And there appeared to them Moses and Elijah talking about Jesus.
And Peter said to Jesus: "Lord, here we are well: if you want, we will set up three tents here: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was still speaking, a shining cloud covered them, and a voice from the cloud said: "This is my beloved son, in whom I am well pleased; listen to him!"
When the students heard this, they fell on their faces and were very scared.
But Jesus came and said to them, "Get up, do not be afraid."
And when they lifted their eyes, they saw only Jesus alone.
And when they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, "Tell no one about the vision you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
And the disciples asked him: "So why does the book of the Jews say that Elijah must come first?"
Answer them: "Truly, Elijah is coming and he will restore all things."
But I will take you to Elijah, and they did not recognize him, but they did to him whatever they wanted. And I, the Son of Man, will also suffer at their hands.»
At that time, the students understood that he was talking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy possessed by a demon (Mark 9:14-29; Luke 9:37-43)
When Jesus and his disciples came to the people, a man came and knelt before Jesus and said:
Oh Lord, have mercy on my son, because he faints and suffers a lot and always falls into fire and water.
And I brought him to your students, but they couldn't see him.
And Jesus answered and said: "O corrupt generation, woe to you for having no faith! How long shall I stay with you? How long shall I endure you? Bring him here to me!"
Jesus cast out the demon, and it came out of him, and the boy was healed immediately.
At that time, the students came to Jesus aside and said, "Why couldn't we cast it out?"
He said to them: "Because of your weak faith! I tell you the truth, if you had faith as small as a mustard seed, you would say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there!' and it would move, and you would become capable of everything."
# 17:21 There are ancient texts containing this verse: This kind does not come out except by prayer and fasting. Jesus speaks again about his death and resurrection (Mark 9:30-32; Luke 9:43-45)
When the disciples were gathered in Galilee, Jesus said to them: "I, the Son of Man, will be delivered into the hands of men,
"And they will kill me, and I will rise on the third day." You all worry too much. Jesus pays the temple taxes.
They went to Capernaum to those who collect the temple taxes for Peter and said to him, "Doesn't your teacher pay the taxes?"
He answered: "Yes, indeed." And when I went home, Jesus spoke first and said: "What do you think, Simon? Where do the kings of the earth get taxes and levies from? From their own people or from foreigners?"
Peter said: "From the Brahmins." Jesus said: "Well then, the children of the country are free."
But we don't want to cause them any trouble. Go to the lake and cast your fishing line, and catch the first fish you get. When you open its mouth, you'll find a silver coin enough to pay for both of us. Take it and pay my taxes and yours.
